# Pegasus

> Pegasus is a generative model. It analyzes multiple modalities to generate contextually relevant text.

Pegasus is a generative model for video-to-text generation. Pegasus analyzes multiple modalities to generate contextually relevant text based on the content of your videos.

# Available versions

#### [Pegasus 1.6](/v1.3/docs/concepts/models/pegasus/pegasus-1-6)

Analyze videos or images to generate text, with egocentric video understanding, improved entity recognition, and in-segment events.

#### [Pegasus 1.5](/v1.3/docs/concepts/models/pegasus/pegasus-1-5)

Analyze videos to generate summaries, detailed descriptions, and structured responses.

# Examples

## Segmenting egocentric video into atomic actions

This example request segments first-person factory footage into atomic actions and labels the hand used for each one. The output is truncated to the first three segments.

**`Request`**

```json title="Request" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=20
{
  "model_name": "pegasus1.6",
  "analysis_mode": "time_based_metadata",
  "video": {
    "type": "asset_id",
    "asset_id": "<YOUR_ASSET_ID>"
  },
  "temperature": 0,
  "min_segment_duration": 2,
  "max_tokens": 98304,
  "response_format": {
    "type": "segment_definitions",
    "segment_definitions": [
      {
        "id": "atomic_action",
        "description": "Cut this egocentric video into atomic actions. One segment is ONE uninterrupted motion by one hand, or by both hands when they act on the same object together. Cut a NEW segment at EVERY change in what a hand is doing — a new contact, a release, a re-grasp, a change of direction, a change of object, a change of which hand is acting, a correction, a nudge, a pause. Small and incidental motions count: a two-second re-grip is its own segment. Never summarise several motions into one segment, and never skip a motion because it is brief. A repeated activity is one segment per repetition, never one segment for the whole run. Cover the clip end to end — consecutive segments touch, each starting where the previous one ended, with no gaps and no overlaps — and hold the same level of detail in the last minute as in the first.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "action",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Exactly ONE action: the single motion this segment covers, as verb + object + hand. Never a list and never a sequence — if you are about to join two verbs with 'and' or a comma, that is two segments, not one. Name the motion, not its purpose."
          },
          {
            "name": "hands_used",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Which hand performs THIS action. Use 'both' only when the two hands act on the same object at the same moment and neither could do it alone. If one hand acts while the other only holds, steadies, supports or waits, the value is the ACTING hand alone, not 'both'. Use 'none' when neither hand is manipulating anything. Asymmetric work is the norm, so do not default to 'both' because two hands are visible.",
            "enum": ["left", "right", "both", "none"]
          },
          {
            "name": "description",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One sentence describing this single motion in full."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**`Output`**

```json title="Output" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=20
{
  "atomic_action": [
    {
      "start_time": 0,
      "end_time": 2,
      "metadata": {
        "action": "hold screwdriver",
        "hands_used": "right",
        "description": "The right hand holds the pneumatic screwdriver steady while the left hand holds a small screw on the workbench."
      }
    },
    {
      "start_time": 2,
      "end_time": 5,
      "metadata": {
        "action": "hold screwdriver",
        "hands_used": "right",
        "description": "The right hand holds the pneumatic screwdriver steady while the left hand holds a small screw on the workbench."
      }
    },
    {
      "start_time": 5,
      "end_time": 7,
      "metadata": {
        "action": "reach for motor",
        "hands_used": "left",
        "description": "The left hand reaches forward and grasps the motor assembly to pull it closer to the center of the workspace."
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Labeling task attempts in an egocentric cooking video

This example request segments a first-person cooking video into task attempts and states the goal of each attempt.

**`Request`**

```json title="Request" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=20
{
  "model_name": "pegasus1.6",
  "analysis_mode": "time_based_metadata",
  "video": {
    "type": "asset_id",
    "asset_id": "<YOUR_ASSET_ID>"
  },
  "temperature": 0,
  "min_segment_duration": 10,
  "max_tokens": 98304,
  "response_format": {
    "type": "segment_definitions",
    "segment_definitions": [
      {
        "id": "task",
        "description": "A coherent task attempt by the camera-wearer, from the first action that begins it to the point where its goal is achieved or abandoned. Do not split a task into its constituent motions — one segment per task attempt. Example boundaries: the whole of 'pack one shirt into a box', not the reach, the fold and the place separately.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "goal",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Imperative phrasing of the task goal, as a language command would be given to a robot, e.g. 'fold the shirt and place it in the box'. Keep it short — a command, not a narration. Under 60 characters, no 'the person…', no account of what happened."
          },
          {
            "name": "description",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "What the camera-wearer actually did, in one sentence: the objects involved and the order of the steps. Say what the goal does not already say — never restate the goal in the third person. Maximum 200 characters."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**`Output`**

```json title="Output" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=20
