## Auditing privacy exposure in egocentric video

This example request flags faces, bystanders, documents, and screens in first-person factory footage for a privacy review. The output is truncated to one segment per definition.

**`Request`**

```json title="Request" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=20
{
  "model_name": "pegasus1.6",
  "analysis_mode": "time_based_metadata",
  "video": {
    "type": "asset_id",
    "asset_id": "<YOUR_ASSET_ID>"
  },
  "temperature": 0,
  "min_segment_duration": 2,
  "max_tokens": 98304,
  "response_format": {
    "type": "segment_definitions",
    "segment_definitions": [
      {
        "id": "face",
        "description": "A stretch of time in which a human face is visible in frame. Start a new segment each time a face enters frame and end it when no face remains. Do not create a segment when no face is visible.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "identifiable",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Whether the face is visible clearly enough and long enough that the person could be recognised from this footage. Use 'partially' when the face is turned, blurred by motion, or only briefly in frame.",
            "enum": ["yes", "partially", "no"]
          },
          {
            "name": "description",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One sentence stating where in the frame the face appears and what makes it identifiable or not."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "bystander",
        "description": "A stretch of time in which a person other than the camera-wearer is visible in frame, whether or not their face can be seen. Start a new segment each time such a person enters frame. Do not create a segment for the camera-wearer's own hands or body.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "position",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Where in the frame the person is, relative to the work the camera-wearer is doing.",
            "enum": ["foreground", "background", "edge-of-frame"]
          },
          {
            "name": "description",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One sentence stating what is visible of this person and what they appear to be doing."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "document",
        "description": "A stretch of time in which printed or handwritten text on paper, card, or a label is visible in frame. Do not create a segment for text on an electronic display.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "legible",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Whether the text could actually be read from this footage, as opposed to being recognisable as text.",
            "enum": ["yes", "partially", "no"]
          },
          {
            "name": "description",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One sentence stating what the document appears to be and whether any of its text can be made out."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "screen",
        "description": "A stretch of time in which an electronic display is visible in frame. Start a new segment each time a display enters frame.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "legible",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Whether what the display is showing could actually be read from this footage.",
            "enum": ["yes", "partially", "no"]
          },
          {
            "name": "description",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One sentence stating what kind of display it is and what, if anything, can be made out on it."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**`Output`**

```json title="Output" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=20
{
  "face": [
    {
      "start_time": 27,
      "end_time": 29,
      "metadata": {
        "identifiable": "partially",
        "description": "A face is visible in the upper left of the frame, seen in profile looking down at the workbench, but it is partially obscured by the angle and motion blur."
      }
    }
  ],
  "bystander": [
    {
      "start_time": 27,
      "end_time": 30,
      "metadata": {
        "position": "foreground",
        "description": "A person wearing a black shirt and a head-mounted camera is visible on the left side of the frame, leaning over the workspace and reaching toward the phone with their right hand."
      }
    }
  ],
  "screen": [
    {
      "start_time": 28,
      "end_time": 30,
      "metadata": {
        "legible": "no",
        "description": "A smartphone screen displaying a chat interface with green message bubbles, but the text is too blurry to read."
      }
    }
  ],
  "document": [
    {
      "start_time": 42,
      "end_time": 44,
      "metadata": {
        "legible": "no",
        "description": "A white paper label or document is visible on the right side of the frame, but the text is too blurry and out of focus to be read."
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Summarizing educational videos

This example prompt summarizes an educational video without any customization.

**`Prompt`**

```markdown title="Prompt" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=5
Summarize this video
```

**`Output`**

```markdown title="Output" showLineNumbers={false} wordWrap maxLines=5
This video presents an educational overview of advanced techniques for adapting pre-trained large language models (LLMs) to specialized tasks, with a focus on prompt tuning as a more efficient alternative to traditional fine-tuning and manual prompt engineering. The speaker, Martin Kevner, Master Inventor at IBM, begins by explaining that foundation models like ChatGPT are highly flexible but may need customization for specific applications. He contrasts three approaches: fine-tuning, which requires large amounts of labeled data and retraining; prompt engineering, where humans craft explicit instructions (hard prompts) to guide model behavior; and prompt tuning, which uses AI-generated, tunable soft prompts—numerical embeddings that are invisible to humans but highly effective. The video highlights that soft prompts outperform human-designed ones, enable faster adaptation, and are particularly valuable in multi-task and continual learning scenarios, where models must switch tasks or learn new concepts without forgetting prior knowledge. Despite the efficiency and power of prompt tuning, the speaker notes its lack of interpretability, as the AI cannot explain why certain embeddings were chosen. The video concludes with a reflective tone, acknowledging that while prompt engineering may become obsolete, the future lies in leveraging the underlying embedding layers, where "a string of numbers is worth a thousand words." The presentation is visually enhanced with dynamic digital annotations, including animated text, virtual writing, and diagrams illustrating model architectures and data flow, reinforcing the technical concepts through interactive visualization.
```

